/*
=============================================================================
  Weekly SQL Challenge 02
=============================================================================
  Database : grocery_db schema
  Tables   : transactions, product_areas

  TASK
  ----
  Identify the top 3 product areas based on the highest total items sold.

  EXPECTED OUTPUT
  ---------------
  Three rows with columns:
    - product_area_name  : the name of the product area (e.g. Dairy, Bakery)
    - total_items        : total number of items sold in that area
  Rows must be ordered by total_items in DESCENDING order (highest first).

=============================================================================
  BUSINESS CONTEXT
=============================================================================
  Imagine you are a data analyst at a mid-size grocery chain competing
  against large retailers like Walmart and Costco.  Leadership wants to
  make data-driven decisions to:

    • Allocate shelf space more profitably — put the most-sold categories
      front-and-centre to maximise basket size.
    • Plan promotions strategically — knowing the top 3 product areas lets
      the marketing team focus discount campaigns where customer demand is
      already high, increasing basket conversion rates.
    • Manage inventory and supply chains — high-volume areas need tighter
      stock control to avoid costly out-of-stock events.
    • Benchmark against competitors — if "Beverages" is your #1 area but a
      rival is out-performing you on "Snacks", that gap signals an
      opportunity or a threat to profitability.

  This single query answers: "Where are customers spending the most?"
  That insight directly informs purchasing budgets, staffing levels in
  those aisles, and which suppliers to negotiate better pricing with.
=============================================================================
*/

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SELECT clause
-- What it does: specifies the exact columns we want returned.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
select

  -- b.product_area_name
  --   'b' is the alias we gave the product_areas table (see FROM below).
  --   product_area_name holds a human-readable label such as "Dairy" or
  --   "Bakery".  We retrieve it here so the final result is meaningful
  --   rather than just showing a numeric ID.
  b.product_area_name,

  -- sum(a.num_items) as total_items
  --   'a' is the alias for the transactions table.
  --   num_items stores how many items were bought in each transaction row.
  --   SUM() is an aggregate function — it adds up all num_items values
  --   that belong to the same product area after the GROUP BY is applied.
  --   'as total_items' renames the calculated column to a clear label in
  --   the output — a best practice so downstream users know what the
  --   number represents.
  --
  --   BUSINESS VALUE: This number tells us the raw volume moved per
  --   product area, which is a leading indicator of revenue and footfall.
  sum(a.num_items) as total_items

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FROM clause + alias
-- What it does: declares the primary (driving) table for the query.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
from

  -- grocery_db.transactions a
  --   grocery_db  : the database (schema) name — keeps queries portable
  --                 across environments (dev, staging, prod).
  --   transactions: the table that records every purchase event.
  --                 Each row typically represents one product line within
  --                 a customer's basket.
  --   a           : a short alias so we don't have to type the full table
  --                 name every time we reference its columns.
  grocery_db.transactions a

  -- INNER JOIN grocery_db.product_areas b
  --   Combines the transactions table with the product_areas lookup table.
  --   INNER JOIN returns only rows where a matching record exists in BOTH
  --   tables — any transaction with an unrecognised product_area_id would
  --   be silently excluded, keeping results clean.
  --
  --   'b' is the alias for product_areas.
  --
  -- ON a.product_area_id = b.product_area_id
  --   This is the JOIN condition — the "bridge" between the two tables.
  --   a.product_area_id  : numeric foreign key stored on each transaction.
  --   b.product_area_id  : numeric primary key in the product_areas table.
  --   Matching these two columns links every transaction to its
  --   human-readable category name.
  --
  --   WHY THIS MATTERS: Without the join, we would only have numeric IDs
  --   in the output.  Business users need "Bakery" not "3".
  inner join grocery_db.product_areas b on a.product_area_id = b.product_area_id

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- GROUP BY clause
-- What it does: collapses all transaction rows into one summary row
--               per unique product_area_name.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
group by

  -- b.product_area_name
  --   Before GROUP BY, the result set has one row per transaction
  --   (potentially millions of rows).  GROUP BY merges all rows that share
  --   the same product_area_name into a single row, which is what allows
  --   SUM() to total up the items across all transactions in that area.
  --
  --   ANALOGY: Think of sorting receipts into labelled folders (one folder
  --   per product area) and then counting everything inside each folder.
  b.product_area_name

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ORDER BY clause
-- What it does: sorts the grouped results before they are returned.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
order by

  -- total_items desc
  --   Sorts by the alias we defined in the SELECT clause.
  --   DESC = descending, so the product area with the MOST items sold
  --   appears first (rank #1 at the top).
  --
  --   BUSINESS VALUE: Decision-makers expect a ranked list — "show me
  --   the best performers first."  Descending order makes the top
  --   opportunities immediately visible without manual scanning.
  total_items desc

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- LIMIT clause
-- What it does: caps the number of rows returned by the query.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

-- limit 3
--   Returns only the first 3 rows after sorting — i.e. the TOP 3
--   product areas by items sold.
--
--   WHY 3?  The business question is specifically about the top 3 so
--   leadership can focus resources on a manageable shortlist.  In a
--   real scenario this number might be a dashboard parameter that
--   users can change (e.g. top 5 or top 10).
--
--   NOTE: LIMIT is applied AFTER ORDER BY, so the 3 rows we keep are
--   guaranteed to be the highest-ranked ones.
limit 3;

/*
